  "hints": [
    "Readings can be negative. If you start `best` at 0, a log of all-negative readings breaks it. Start from the first window's total instead.",
    "Slide the window: start with the sum of the first K. Moving one step right, add the new reading and subtract the one that just left. (A loop inside a loop that re-adds each window also works at this size.)",
    "Track the best sum and its start. Only replace it on a strictly bigger sum (`>`), so ties keep the earliest. Print `i + 1` for the 1-based position."
  ],
  "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint readings[1000];\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n, k;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", &n, &k);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        scanf(\"%d\", &readings[i]);\n    }\n    int *p = readings;\n    long long window = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) window += *(p + i);\n    long long best = window;\n    int start = 0;\n    for (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n        window += *(p + i) - *(p + i - k);\n        if (window > best) {\n            best = window;\n            start = i - k + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    printf(\"sum %lld at %d\\n\", best, start + 1);\n    return 0;\n}\n"
}
